{"name":"Chicory Green","hex":"#bbab75","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chicory","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Either of two plants of the Asteraceae family.\n\n Common chicory (Cichorium intybus), source of Belgian endive, radicchio, and certain other cultivars with other names.","Common chicory (Cichorium intybus), source of Belgian endive, radicchio, and certain other cultivars with other names."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chicory"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chicory","blocks":[{"text":"Common chicory is a somewhat woody, perennial herbaceous plant of the family Asteraceae, usually with bright blue flowers, rarely white or pink. Native to Europe, North Africa, and western Asia, it has been introduced to the Americas, Australia, and South Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Illustration_Cichorium_intybus0_clean.jpg/330px-Illustration_Cichorium_intybus0_clean.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicory","wikidata":"Q2544599"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Wegwarte_Cichorium_intybus.jpg/500px-Wegwarte_Cichorium_intybus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Wegwarte_Cichorium_intybus.jpg/500px-Wegwarte_Cichorium_intybus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWegwarte_Cichorium_intybus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Dried_chicory_root.jpg/500px-Dried_chicory_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Dried_chicory_root.jpg/1280px-Dried_chicory_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Dried_chicory_root.jpg/500px-Dried_chicory_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dried chicory root","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADried_chicory_root.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Chicory_tea.jpg/500px-Chicory